The comet's activity is extremely low, with an active area of only ~700 m^2, which is among the lowest values ever measured on a comet. The extremely low level of activity is likely the result of extreme depletion of near-surface volatiles caused by excessive passages in the inner Solar System.

Surveys that focus on twilight directions and deep narrowband imaging can help identify comets with extremely low activity. The detection of P/2021 HS highlights the challenges of detecting comets with extremely low activity levels and emphasizes the importance of observing comets at high phase angles and using deep narrowband imaging to isolate the signal of emission lines/bands.
