                \begin{document}$$w^2$$\end{document}$. We also investigate the similarities and differences of the universality if a word *w* is repeated or $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$w^R$$\end{document}$ and $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\pi (w)$$\end{document}$ resp. are appended to *w*, for a morphic permutation of the alphabet $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\pi $$\end{document}$. As consequences, we get a linear run-time algorithm for computing a minimal length scattered factor of *ww* that is not a scattered factor of *w*. This approach works for arbitrary alphabets, while, e.g., the approach of \[[@CR17]\] only works for binary ones. We conclude the section by analysing the new notion of *k*-circular universality, connected to the universality of repetitions.

In Sect. [5](#Sec5){ref-type="sec"} we consider the problem of modifying the universality of a word by repeated concatenations or deletions. Motivated by the fact that, in general, starting from an input word *w*, we could reach larger sets of scattered factors of fixed length by iterative concatenations of *w*, we show that, for a word *w* a positive integer *k*, we can compute efficiently the minimal $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\ell $$\end{document}$ such that $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$w^\ell $$\end{document}$ is *k*-universal. This result is extensible to sets of words. Finally, the shortest prefix or suffix we need to delete to lower the universality index of a word to a given number can be computed in linear time. Interestingly, in all of the algorithms where we are concerned with reaching *k*-universality we never effectively construct a *k*-universal word (which would take exponential time, when *k* is given as input via its binary encoding, and would have been needed when solving these problems using, e.g., \[[@CR10], [@CR11]\]). Our algorithms run in polynomial time w.r.t. \|*w*\|, the length of the input word, and $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\log _2 k$$\end{document}$, the size of the representation of *k*.

