Introduction {#Sec1}
============

A scattered factor (also called subsequence or subword) of a given word *w* is a word *u* such that there exist (possibly empty) words $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \usepackage{amsmath}
                \usepackage{wasysym} 
                \usepackage{amsfonts} 
                \usepackage{amssymb} 
                \usepackage{amsbsy}
                \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                \usepackage{upgreek}
                \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                \begin{document}$$v_0, \ldots , v_n, u_1, \ldots , u_n$$\end{document}$ with $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$u = u_1 \ldots u_n$$\end{document}$ and $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$w = v_0 u_1 v_1 u_2 \ldots u_n v_n$$\end{document}$. Thus, scattered factors of a word *w* are imperfect representations of *w*, obtained by removing some of its parts. As such, there is considerable interest in the relationship between a word and its scattered factors, both from a theoretical and practical point of view (cf. e.g., the chapter *Subwords* by J. Sakarovitch and I. Simon in \[[@CR27], Chapter 6\] for an introduction to the combinatorial properties). Indeed, in situations where one has to deal with input strings in which errors may occur, e.g., sequencing DNA or transmitting a digital signal, scattered factors form a natural model for the processed data as parts of the input may be missing. This versatility of scattered factors is also highlighted by the many contexts in which this concept appears. For instance, in \[[@CR16], [@CR24], [@CR37]\], various logic-theories were developed around the notion of scattered factors which are analysed mostly with automata theory tools and discussed in connection to applications in formal verification. On an even more fundamental perspective, there have been efforts to bridge the gap between the field of combinatorics on words, with its usual non-commutative tools, and traditional linear algebra, via, e.g., subword histories or Parikh matrices (cf. e.g., \[[@CR30], [@CR33], [@CR34]\]) which are algebraic structures in which the number of specific scattered factors occurring in a word are stored. In an algorithmic framework, scattered factors are central in many classical problems, e.g., the longest common subsequence or the shortest common supersequence problems \[[@CR1], [@CR28]\], the string-to-string correction problem \[[@CR36]\], as well as in bioinformatics-related works \[[@CR10]\].

In this paper we focus, for a given word, on the sets of scattered factors of a given length: the (full) *k*-spectrum of *w* is the set containing all scattered factors of *w* of length exactly *k* (up to *k* resp.). The total set of scattered factors (also called downward closure) of $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$w = \mathtt {a}\mathtt {b}\mathtt {a}$$\end{document}$ is $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\{\varepsilon , \mathtt {a}, \mathtt {a}\mathtt {a}, \mathtt {a}\mathtt {b}, \mathtt {a}\mathtt {b}\mathtt {a}, \mathtt {b}, \mathtt {b}\mathtt {a}\}$$\end{document}$ and the 2-spectrum is $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\{ \mathtt {a}\mathtt {a}, \mathtt {a}\mathtt {b}, \mathtt {b}\mathtt {a}\}$$\end{document}$. The study of scattered factors of a fixed length of a word has its roots in \[[@CR35]\], where the relation $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\sim _k$$\end{document}$ (called Simon's congruence) defines the congruence of words that have the same full *k*-spectra. Our main interest here lies in a special congruence class w.r.t. $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\sim _k$$\end{document}$: the class of words which have the largest possible *k*-spectrum. A word *w* is called *k-universal* if its *k*-spectrum contains all the words of length *k* over a given alphabet. That is, *k*-universal words are those words that are as rich as possible in terms of scattered factors of length *k* (and, consequently, also scattered factors of length at most *k*): the restriction of their downward closure to words of length *k* contains all possible words of the respective length, i.e., is a *universal* language. Thus $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$w=\mathtt {a}\mathtt {b}\mathtt {a}$$\end{document}$ is not 2-universal since $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\mathtt {b}\mathtt {b}$$\end{document}$ is not a scattered factor of *w*, while $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$w'=\mathtt {a}\mathtt {b}\mathtt {a}\mathtt {b}$$\end{document}$ is 2-universal. Calling a words *universal* if its *k*-spectrum contains all possible words of length *k*, is rooted in formal language theory. The classical universality problem (cf. e.g., \[[@CR18]\]) is whether a given language *L* (over an alphabet $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\varSigma $$\end{document}$) is equal to $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\varSigma ^{*}$$\end{document}$, where *L* can be given, e.g., as the language accepted by an automaton. A variant of this problem, called length universality, asks, for a natural number $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\ell $$\end{document}$ and a language *L* (over $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\varSigma $$\end{document}$), whether *L* contains all strings of length $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\ell $$\end{document}$ over $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\varSigma $$\end{document}$. See \[[@CR14]\] for a series of results on this problem and a discussion on its motivation, and \[[@CR14], [@CR23], [@CR31]\] and the references therein for more results on the universality problem for various types of automata. The universality problem was also considered for words \[[@CR6], [@CR29]\] and, more recently, for partial words \[[@CR2], [@CR15]\] w.r.t. their factors. In this context, the question is to find, for a given $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\ell $$\end{document}$, a word *w* over an alphabet $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\varSigma $$\end{document}$, such that each word of length $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\varSigma $$\end{document}$ occurs exactly once as a contiguous factor of *w*. De Bruijn sequences \[[@CR6]\] fulfil this property, and have been shown to have many applications in various areas of computer science or combinatorics, see \[[@CR2], [@CR15]\] and the references therein. As such, our study of scattered factor-universality is related to, and motivated by, this well developed and classical line of research.

While $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\sim _k$$\end{document}$ is a well studied congruence relation from language theoretic, combinatorial, or algorithmic points of view (see \[[@CR11], [@CR27], [@CR35]\] and the references therein), the study of universality w.r.t. scattered factors seems to have been mainly carried out from a language theoretic point of view. In \[[@CR20]\] as well as in \[[@CR21], [@CR22]\] the authors approach, in the context of studying the height of piecewise testable languages, the notion of $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\ell $$\end{document}$-rich words, which coincides with the $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$\ell $$\end{document}$-universal words we define here; we will discuss the relation between these notions, as well as our preference to talk about universality rather than richness, later in the paper. A combinatorial study of scattered factors universality was started in \[[@CR5]\], where a simple characterisation of *k*-universal binary words was given. In the combinatorics on words literature, more attention was given to the so called binomial complexity of words, i.e., a measure of the multiset of scattered factors that occur in a word, where each occurrence of such a factor is considered as an element of the respective multiset (see, e.g., \[[@CR12], [@CR25], [@CR26], [@CR32]\]). As such, it seemed interesting to us to continue the work on scattered factor universality: try to understand better (in general, not only in the case of binary alphabets) their combinatorial properties, but, mainly, try to develop an algorithmic toolbox around the concept of (*k*-)universal words.

**Our Results.** In the preliminaries we give the basic definitions and recall the arch factorisation introduced by Hebrard \[[@CR17]\]. Moreover we explain in detail the connection to richness introduced in \[[@CR20]\].

In Sect. [3](#Sec3){ref-type="sec"} we show one of our main results: testing whether two words have the same full *k*-spectrum, for given $\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                \begin{document}$$k\in \mathbb {N}$$\end{document}$, can be done in optimal linear time for words over ordered alphabets and improve and extend the results of \[[@CR11]\]. They also lead to an optimal solution over general alphabets.

